# Optional: serve the torrent gateway under the main hostname.
#
# NOT ENABLED. Paste this block inside the yarrit.com site block in
# stream/Caddyfile, then reload Caddy, only after deciding the question below.
#
# WHAT IT FIXES
# The gateway is what lets a device play a torrent without being a torrent
# client itself: it joins the swarm and serves the file over ordinary HTTP with
# range requests. Roku and Xbox cannot torrent on their own, so it is the only
# way those devices play anything that is not a direct archive.org download.
#
# Today the Roku reaches it at http://192.168.0.118:8900 -- a private address
# over plain HTTP. That works in one house and nowhere else. Routing it here
# puts it behind the same hostname and certificate as everything else, so any
# device anywhere can use it and a self-hoster's clients find it at their own
# address with no extra configuration.
#
# THE DECISION THIS REQUIRES
# Right now the VPS relays search metadata only; media bytes never touch it.
# Enabling this changes that: torrent traffic would flow through the VPS, from
# its IP, in the operator's name. That is a different legal posture, not a
# technical detail, and it is worth deciding deliberately rather than
# discovering later.
#
# A self-hoster enabling this on their own box carries that risk themselves,
# which is the whole point of the project being self-hostable.

handle /gw/* {
	# The gateway serves whole video files, so the usual proxy buffering and
	# timeouts are wrong here -- a two-hour film is one long response.
	reverse_proxy 192.168.0.118:8900 {
		flush_interval -1
		transport http {
			response_header_timeout 120s
			read_timeout 0
		}
	}
}
